<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  CONNECTING THE SIDES WHILE HE DRAWS, and the line moving when they connect.

  His words: "make it easier to connect the sides when I draw", and then, on
  seeing the first version: "When I attach them together, I need to see the line
  move there too so there is no confusion, need it to be more seamless than
  that."

  A SEPARATE FILE, not strings.xml, on purpose: strings.xml is edited by several
  waves at once and two agents in one file loses an edit. Android merges every
  values/*.xml, so these resolve exactly as if they were in it.

  WRITTEN IN WHAT IT COSTS HIM, never in what the code does. Not "create
  joint", but one post instead of two where the sides meet, one less cap, one less
  bag. He is deciding whether to tap, in a yard, in sunlight, holding a phone in
  one hand.
-->
<resources>

    <!-- THE STANDING OFFER, beside the drawing, after a point lands on another
         side's end. One tap takes it; anything else leaves it. Carrying on
         drawing is never a yes. -->
    <string name="snap_join_offer">These two sides meet here. Make it one post?</string>
    <string name="snap_join_offer_saving">One post instead of two: a post, its cap and its concrete come off the order.</string>
    <string name="snap_join_offer_action">One post</string>

    <!-- The whole offer as one snackbar line, for the version of the screen
         that has no room for two. Kept as its own string rather than joined in
         code, so a translator can put the saving first if that reads better. -->
    <string name="snap_join_offer_line">These two sides meet here. Make it one post? A post, its cap and its concrete come off the order.</string>

    <!-- ATTACHING FROM THE ATTACH TOOL, where the two ends may not be together
         yet. The side that moves is named, and so is what it does to the
         footage, because footage is labour. %1$s is the side, %2$s the distance. -->
    <string name="attach_moves">%1$s moves %2$s to meet it, so the two ends become one point on the plan.</string>
    <!-- %1$s is the side, %2$s its footage now, %3$s its footage after. -->
    <string name="attach_moves_footage">%1$s measures %2$s now and %3$s after, so its labour follows.</string>
    <!-- Nothing to move: the drawing already shows them meeting. -->
    <string name="attach_already_together">These two ends are already on one point, so nothing on the plan moves.</string>

    <!-- Too far apart to close. Says what to do instead, in the gesture he
         already knows. %1$s is the distance. -->
    <string name="attach_refused_too_far">Those two ends are %1$s apart. Attaching them would drag one side that far and change its length, so it is not done from here: drag the end over to the other corner first — it snaps onto it — and the app will offer to make it one post.</string>

    <!-- Detach is the opposite of attach about the POST, not about the drawing.
         Said in the dialog so it is never a surprise. -->
    <string name="attach_detach_keeps_drawing">The line stays where it is. Each side gets its own post back; move the corner yourself if it should come apart on the plan too.</string>

</resources>
